"""Domain 5 rows: automation, response, governance, risk, compliance, awareness."""
from curriculum_sec.rowdata import R

ROWS = [
    R(25, '4.7.provision-auto', 'Automated provisioning',
      'Automation can create and remove user and resource access from a ticket so it happens the same way every time. The workflow must include removal. A script that only adds access grows the blast radius',
      'A joiner workflow creates the account and the groups from HR data. The leaver workflow disables them the same day. A script that only runs on hire leaves every departure behind',
      'Automation of a bad process makes the bad process faster',
      'factory line', 'The line has to include the step that takes the badge back',
      'Automate join and leave',
      'Removal is part of the workflow', 'Only automate the add', 'A script with no owner'),
    R(25, '4.7.guard', 'Guard rails and security groups',
      'Guard rails stop a deployment that breaks a rule, such as a public storage bucket or a security group open to the world. They are preventive automation. Someone must own the exception path',
      'A pipeline blocks a security group that allows SSH from anywhere. The developer asks for an exception. You require the jump host instead. The rail did the job',
      'A guard rail you can click past with no record is a suggestion',
      'safety interlock', 'The machine will not start with the guard open, unless a named person overrides it',
      'Block the known-bad deploy',
      'Fail the build when the rule breaks', 'A warning nobody reads', 'Open SSH so the deploy succeeds'),
    R(25, '4.7.tickets-auto', 'Ticket creation and escalation',
      'Automation can open a ticket from an alert and escalate if nobody accepts it. The ticket needs the evidence and a severity. A thousand auto-tickets with no owner are noise',
      'A high alert opens a ticket and pages after ten minutes of silence. A low alert opens a ticket and does not page. You do not page a human for every failed login',
      'Escalation without a human at the end is a circle',
      'dispatch', 'The radio creates the call and bumps it if nobody answers',
      'Page only what a person must touch now',
      'Evidence, severity, and a person', 'Page on every alert', 'Tickets with no owner'),
    R(25, '4.7.services', 'Enabling and disabling by automation',
      'Automation can disable a leaked account or a hot host faster than a person. The action needs a guard so a bad rule does not disable the identity provider or the on-call tool',
      'A stolen token triggers a disable of that user and a revoke of sessions. The rule refuses to disable the break-glass account. You test the revoke in a lab before you trust it at 2 a.m.',
      'An untested disable script is a new outage waiting for a false positive',
      'emergency stop', 'The stop button is tested, and it does not cut the lights in the operating room',
      'Fast disable, with a protected exception',
      'Test it. Protect break-glass', 'Auto-disable the identity provider', 'Never automate a disable'),
    R(25, '4.7.ci', 'Continuous integration and testing',
      'Continuous integration builds and tests every change. Security tests belong in that line: dependency checks, secret scans, and policy checks. A secret committed and then built is already copied',
      'The pipeline fails when a key appears in the diff. The developer removes it and rotates the key, because the commit already replicated. The scan is early, not a yearly audit',
      'A green build that does not look for secrets is incomplete',
      'inspection on the line', 'You catch the bad part before it ships in every unit',
      'Scan secrets and dependencies on every change',
      'Fail the build and rotate the leaked key', 'A yearly scan is enough', 'The secret is fine if the repo is private'),
    R(25, '4.7.api', 'Integrations and APIs',
      'Integrations and APIs connect tools with tokens. Each token is a credential. Give it the smallest scope, rotate it, and do not put it in a script on a share. Log the calls',
      'A chat bot token can read every channel and is stored in a wiki. You rotate it, store it in a vault, and cut the scope to one channel. The wiki copy is the incident',
      'An API key is not a low-risk setting. It is a password for a program',
      'service door key', 'The key is for one door, and it is not taped to the wiki',
      'Small scope, vault, rotation',
      'Treat tokens as secrets', 'A wiki is a fine key store', 'Broad tokens are easier so they are better'),
    R(25, '4.7.benefits', 'Benefits of careful automation',
      'Good automation saves time, enforces the baseline, makes infrastructure standard, scales without a new exception each time, and shortens reaction. It can also help people stay, because the toil drops. Those benefits appear only if the automation is maintained',
      'Account removal that used to take a week happens in an hour, the same way, and the on-call is not hand-editing fifty consoles. That is the benefit you can measure',
      'A slide that says efficiency is not the benefit. The measured change is',
      'power tools', 'The shop is faster when the tools are sharp and someone maintains them',
      'Measure the time and the consistency',
      'Same process, faster, with less toil', 'Automation is good because the slide says so', 'No one has to understand it'),
    R(25, '4.7.risks', 'Complexity, cost, and single points of failure',
      'Automation adds complexity, cost, and new single points of failure. A pipeline or an identity bot that nobody can fix is now critical infrastructure. Technical debt is the scripts you are afraid to change',
      'The leaver script breaks and nobody knows the language it was written in. Leavers stop being removed. You document it, you add a second owner, and you keep a manual fallback',
      'A single unowned script is not free. It is a fragile control',
      'one generator', 'The fancy generator is now the only power, and one person knows it',
      'Give automation an owner and a manual fallback',
      'A second person and a fallback', 'The script is too clever to touch', 'Automation cannot fail'),
    R(25, '4.7.support', 'Ongoing supportability',
      'If you cannot patch it, explain it, and run it at 2 a.m., it is not supportable. Prefer boring tools the team can operate over a stack that only the integrator understands',
      'A purchased orchestration platform has no admin training and the contract is ending. You do not add more workflows to it. You move the one critical workflow to a tool the team can run',
      'Supportability is a security requirement because unsupported automation will be bypassed',
      'vehicle you can repair', 'A car nobody can get parts for will be abandoned on the road',
      'The team must be able to run it',
      'Boring and owned beats clever and opaque', 'The integrator will always be there', 'Add workflows to a dying platform'),
    R(25, '4.8.prepare', 'Preparation',
      'Preparation is the work before the incident: contacts, access, logging, backups you have restored, and a plan people have read. You cannot invent access to the logs at 3 a.m.',
      'The on-call cannot get into the SIEM because the only admin is asleep and there is no break-glass. You fix that this week. That fix is preparation',
      'A binder nobody can find is not preparation',
      'packed go-bag', 'The bag is packed before the storm, and someone else knows where it is',
      'Access, contacts, and tested backups beforehand',
      'Prove the on-call can get in', 'We will figure out access during the incident', 'The binder is on one person laptop'),
    R(25, '4.8.detect-analyze', 'Detection and analysis',
      'Detection is noticing. Analysis is deciding what it is, how far it went, and how urgent it is. Analysis uses logs and people. It does not require a perfect story before you contain a spreading fire',
      'An alert fires. You confirm it is not a known backup, you see one host and one account, and you rate it high because the account is payroll. That is enough analysis to act',
      'Analysis forever is a way to avoid a decision',
      'smoke then look', 'You confirm it is smoke and which room, then you move',
      'Enough facts to act, then deepen',
      'Confirm, scope roughly, rate urgency', 'Wait for a perfect narrative', 'Every alert is the same severity'),
    R(25, '4.8.contain', 'Containment',
      'Containment stops the spread while you keep evidence: isolate a host, disable an account, block a token. It is not the same as wiping. Wiping can be eradication, and it can be premature',
      'You disable the account and isolate the laptop from the network. You do not reimage yet because you have not listed the other hosts that received the file',
      'Containment that forgets the cloud token leaves the attacker a door',
      'quarantine', 'You close the ward. You do not burn the chart',
      'Stop the spread. Keep the evidence',
      'Isolate and disable. Do not wipe yet', 'Reimage first', 'Only unplug the office PC'),
    R(25, '4.8.eradicate', 'Eradication and recovery',
      'Eradication removes the cause: the malware, the rogue account, the vulnerable setting. Recovery returns the business from a clean point and watches for the cause to return. Recovering without eradicating restores the attacker',
      'You rebuild the host from a known image, you rotate the passwords the host could see, and you restore data from before the change. Then you watch. Restoring a snapshot that includes the backdoor brings it back',
      'Recovery is not finished when the service is up. It is finished when you would notice a return',
      'remove the mold then repaint', 'Paint over mold and it returns',
      'Remove the cause, then restore clean, then watch',
      'Rotate secrets the host could see', 'Restore the infected snapshot', 'Up means done'),
    R(25, '4.8.lessons', 'Lessons learned',
      'Lessons learned are changes you will actually make: a control, a detection, or a procedure, with an owner and a date. A meeting with no change is a conversation',
      'The review finds that leavers kept VPN access. The lesson is a workflow change owned by IT operations with a date. A slide that says communicate better is not a lesson',
      'Blame is not a lesson. A control change is',
      'after-action note', 'The note names the new step and the person who will add it',
      'A change with an owner',
      'A control change, dated', 'A meeting with no action', 'A public blame note'),
    R(25, '4.8.train-test', 'Training, tabletops, and simulations',
      'Incident training teaches the plan. A tabletop talks through a scenario. A simulation is closer to a live run. People who have never practiced will invent a new plan during the real thing',
      'You run a tabletop where the SIEM is down and the only admin is away. The gap shows up in the room, not at 3 a.m. You fix the break-glass before you claim the plan is trained',
      'A slide deck is not a tabletop',
      'practice fire', 'You walk the stairs once before the building is full of smoke',
      'Practice the ugly case',
      'A scenario that removes the easy tool', 'A slide deck only', 'Skip practice to save time'),
    R(25, '4.8.rca', 'Root cause analysis',
      'Root cause analysis asks why the conditions existed, not only what the attacker clicked. The cause might be a missing process, not a person. You stop when you reach a change that would have prevented the conditions',
      'The phish worked because the executive had no phishing-resistant factor and the payment process trusted email. The root is those two decisions. Retraining one user is not the root fix',
      'Human error as the final cause usually means you stopped too early',
      'five whys on a leak', 'You follow the drip to the failed valve, not only to the person who slipped',
      'Fix the condition, not only the click',
      'Change the process that allowed it', 'Blame the user and stop', 'The malware name is the root cause'),

    R(25, '4.8.authority', 'Authority during an incident',
      'Someone named in the plan has the authority to disconnect a system, disable an account, or call the business owner. Without that authority, the on-call waits while the spread continues',
      'The plan says the incident lead can isolate a host without a change meeting. You use that authority, you write the time, and the review happens after. You do not wait for a committee that is asleep',
      'Authority is not a blank check to wipe every machine',
      'designated driver', 'Someone is named to make the stop decision before the night starts',
      'A named person can contain now',
      'Pre-agreed authority to isolate', 'Wait for a morning meeting', 'Wipe everything under that authority'),
    R(25, '4.8.comms', 'Incident communications',
      'Communications say what happened, what you are doing, and when the next update is, to the people who need it. Speculation and blame do not go in the status note. Customers and regulators are a counsel decision',
      'You post an internal note: mail is isolated, next update in 30 minutes, do not pay any invoice from the compromised thread. You do not name a culprit. Counsel decides any external statement',
      'Silence makes people invent a worse story and click the lure again',
      'status board', 'The board says what is closed and when you will speak again',
      'Facts, next update, no speculation',
      'A timed update without a villain', 'No update until it is over', 'Tweet a theory'),

    R(26, '4.8.hunt', 'Threat hunting',
      'Threat hunting looks for attackers who have not tripped an alert, using a hypothesis: a rare scheduled task, a new service, an odd parent process. It is not the same as waiting for the SIEM',
      'You hypothesize that a silent remote tool would show as a signed binary with an odd parent. You search endpoints. You either find it or you improve the search. You write what you looked for',
      'Hunting without a hypothesis is browsing',
      'looking for a specific track', 'You look for one kind of track, not a random walk',
      'A hypothesis, a search, a note',
      'Write what you hunted', 'Browse the logs with no question', 'Hunting replaces alerts'),
    R(26, '4.8.legalhold', 'Legal hold',
      'A legal hold means you stop deleting data that may be evidence, including the usual retention cleanup. You do not decide the hold alone. Counsel does. You make sure backups and mail are included',
      'Counsel says hold mail for three employees. You suspend deletion for those mailboxes and the backups that would age out. A helpful cleanup that week would have destroyed the record',
      'A hold is not a license to read everything for curiosity',
      'do not shred', 'The shredder stops for the boxes on the list',
      'Stop destruction. Include backups',
      'Counsel names the scope', 'Delete on schedule anyway', 'Read the mail for curiosity'),
    R(26, '4.8.custody', 'Chain of custody',
      'Chain of custody is the record of who had the evidence, when, and what they did. If the record breaks, someone can argue the evidence was altered. Use a log even for a small internal case',
      'You take a laptop, you label it, you record the time and the person, and you store it locked. You do not leave it on a hot desk for the next shift to poke',
      'A photo in a chat is not a chain of custody',
      'evidence label', 'The label and the log travel with the bag',
      'Who had it, when, and what changed',
      'A written handoff', 'A chat photo', 'An unlocked drawer'),
    R(26, '4.8.acquire', 'Acquisition',
      'Acquisition is making a forensic copy without changing the original more than you must. You record hashes of the copy. You work on the copy. You do not browse the original disk out of curiosity',
      'You image the disk, you hash the image, and the analyst mounts the image. The original sits sealed. A quick look that changes timestamps is a problem you now have to explain',
      'A screenshot can be a note. It is not an image of the disk',
      'photograph then glove', 'You photograph the scene before you pick things up',
      'Copy, hash, work on the copy',
      'Leave the original sealed', 'Browse the original', 'A screenshot replaces an image'),
    R(26, '4.8.preserve', 'Preservation and reporting',
      'Preservation keeps evidence unchanged and available. Reporting states facts: what you saw, the hashes, the times, and what you cannot say. Opinion is labeled as opinion',
      'The report says the hash of the image, the time of isolation, and that a remote tool was present. It does not say a named country did it, because you do not have that evidence',
      'A dramatic conclusion without facts is a gift to the other side',
      'lab notebook', 'The notebook separates what was measured from what you guess',
      'Facts, hashes, and the limits of the claim',
      'Say what you can support', 'Name an attacker you cannot prove', 'Skip the hash'),
    R(26, '4.8.ediscovery', 'E-discovery',
      'E-discovery is finding and producing electronic records for a legal process. It is search plus preservation, usually led by counsel. Turning over everything is not helpful. Missing the one mailbox is a failure',
      'Counsel names custodians and a date range. You export those mailboxes and the relevant chats, you hash the export, and you do not add your own commentary folder',
      'E-discovery is not the same as an incident timeline, though they can share sources',
      'records request', 'You produce the files named in the request, complete and unchanged',
      'The named custodians and dates, intact',
      'Follow counsel scope', 'Export the whole company', 'Edit the mail first'),
    R(26, '4.9.logs', 'Which logs to pull',
      'Useful logs include firewall, application, endpoint, operating system security, intrusion detection, network flow, and the metadata around them: who, when, where. One log rarely tells the whole story',
      'The VPN log shows the login, the endpoint log shows the process, and the firewall shows the destination. You pull all three for the same minutes. The application log alone said only error',
      'A log that is not retained cannot be pulled',
      'three cameras', 'The lobby, the hall, and the dock. One camera misses the path',
      'Pull several sources for the same time',
      'Correlate host, network, and app', 'One log is always enough', 'Metadata does not matter'),
    R(26, '4.9.meta', 'Metadata',
      'Metadata is data about data: timestamps, sizes, sender addresses, and which account touched a file. It often solves the who and when without opening the sensitive content',
      'You can show which account copied the folder and when, from the file server audit, without emailing the folder contents to the investigator. You still protect the metadata. It can be sensitive',
      'Metadata is not harmless. A list of who emailed a clinic can be a secret',
      'envelope outside', 'The outside of the envelope answers a lot, and it can still be private',
      'Use it, and protect it',
      'Who and when, without a needless copy of the content', 'Metadata is always public', 'Open every file first'),
    R(26, '4.9.sources', 'Scans, reports, dashboards, and captures',
      'Besides logs, investigations use vulnerability scans, automated reports, dashboards, and packet captures. A capture is the actual traffic and is heavy and sensitive. A dashboard is a view and can hide what it does not graph',
      'The dashboard looks quiet and a short capture on the segment shows a beacon every minute. You keep the capture, you hash it, and you do not capture the whole company for a week just in case',
      'A screenshot of a dashboard is not the underlying events',
      'summary versus tape', 'The summary can hide the one line. The tape is the line, and it is sensitive',
      'Know what the view hides. Capture narrowly',
      'A capture is evidence and is sensitive', 'A green dashboard ends the case', 'Capture everything forever'),
    R(26, '4.9.timeline', 'Building a timeline',
      'A timeline orders facts with sources and times in one zone. It separates what you know from what you guess. It is the handoff. A timeline that skips the containment time hides the most important action',
      'You list detection, isolation, account disable, and the first malicious login, each with the log source and UTC. You mark the suspected initial mail as a hypothesis until the mail log confirms it',
      'Local time on three systems without an offset will invent a false order',
      'storyboard', 'Each frame has a clock and a camera name',
      'Facts, sources, one clock',
      'Label guesses. Use one time zone', 'A story with no times', 'Skip the containment entry'),
    R(26, '4.9.packet', 'Reading a capture safely',
      'A packet capture can show addresses, names, and payloads. Do not capture more than the question requires. Do not post it in a public ticket. Prefer metadata and flows when they answer the question',
      'You need to know which host talked to an address. NetFlow answers it. You do not take a full payload capture of the finance VLAN to answer a who-talked question',
      'A capture can contain passwords and personal data if the traffic was not encrypted',
      'recording a room', 'You do not record every room to see who knocked',
      'The smallest capture that answers the question',
      'Flows first. Payloads only if needed', 'Post the capture in the ticket', 'Capture the whole building'),
    R(26, '4.9.handoff', 'Handing off an investigation',
      'A handoff includes the timeline, the evidence location, the hashes, what was contained, and the next question. The next person should not have to restart from a blank page',
      'You leave the case with the image hash, the ticket of the isolated host, and the unanswered question of whether the token was reused. The next analyst starts there',
      'A verbal briefing with no note is not a handoff',
      'shift change', 'You leave the logbook, not a wave',
      'Timeline, hashes, containment, next question',
      'Write the next question', 'A verbal wave', 'The next person can reread every log'),

    R(27, '5.1.guidelines', 'Guidelines, policies, standards, and procedures',
      'A policy says what must be true. A standard sets the required specific choice. A procedure is how this team does it. A guideline is advice that is not mandatory. Mixing the names makes exceptions impossible to manage',
      'The policy says sensitive data is encrypted. The standard says which product. The procedure is the click path. The guideline suggests a passphrase style. You do not call the click path a policy',
      'A guideline cannot be the thing you audit someone for failing, unless you promote it to a standard',
      'house rules', 'The rule, the required lock model, the how-to, and a tip are four different papers',
      'Keep the four words apart',
      'Policy, standard, procedure, guideline', 'Call everything a policy', 'A guideline is mandatory'),
    R(27, '5.1.policy-set', 'Which policies exist',
      'Expect policies for acceptable use, information security, continuity, disaster recovery, incident response, how software is built, and how changes happen. Each has an owner. A missing incident policy means people invent one during the incident',
      'There is an acceptable use policy and no incident policy. You write the incident one with the response steps and the authority to disconnect a system. You do not wait for a perfect novel',
      'A policy no executive has adopted is a draft',
      'posted rules', 'The rules are adopted, not only drafted',
      'Adopt the policies you will need at 2 a.m.',
      'An adopted incident policy', 'A draft in a folder', 'Acceptable use covers incidents by implication'),
    R(27, '5.1.external', 'External requirements',
      'External requirements come from regulators, law, industry duties, and local, national, or global rules. You list the ones that apply to you. A control that exists only because a law requires it still has to actually operate',
      'A privacy law requires a way for people to ask for their data. You build the request path. Printing the law is not the path',
      'Global customers can pull you into rules from places you do not have an office',
      'local code', 'The building has to meet the code of the city it stands in, and sometimes the customer city',
      'List what applies, and operate it',
      'A working process, not a copied law', 'We are too small for any rule', 'A poster of the regulation'),
    R(27, '5.1.revise', 'Monitoring and revision',
      'Governance includes checking whether the rules are followed and revising them when the business changes. A policy from a decade ago that mentions systems you no longer run will be ignored',
      'You review the remote-access standard after everyone starts hybrid work. The old standard assumed a desk. You update it and you retrain. The review date is on the document',
      'Revision without telling anyone is a secret policy',
      'annual house rules', 'You rewrite the rules when the house changes, and you tell the household',
      'Review on a date, and tell people',
      'A dated review that people hear', 'A ten-year-old document', 'Silent edits'),
    R(27, '5.1.structures', 'Governance structures',
      'Boards set direction and risk appetite. Committees decide cross-team issues. Government entities impose duties if you are in that world. Centralized governance makes one decision. Decentralized governance lets units decide and drift',
      'A security committee with no authority writes advice nobody follows. You give it a decision right for exceptions, or you stop pretending it governs',
      'A committee that cannot say no is a newsletter',
      'town council', 'A council that cannot vote is a conversation club',
      'Someone must be able to say no',
      'Authority, not only advice', 'A newsletter committee', 'Every unit invents its own rules'),
    R(27, '5.1.roles', 'Owners, controllers, processors, custodians',
      'An owner decides. A controller decides why and how personal data is processed. A processor handles it for the controller. A custodian or steward protects and operates the system. Contracts and privacy mail use these words carefully',
      'A cloud vendor processes payroll data for you. You are the controller who decided to collect it. The vendor is the processor. Your IT team is the custodian of the tenant. You do not call the vendor the owner of the employee data',
      'Using the words loosely creates the wrong contract',
      'client and bookkeeper', 'The client decides. The bookkeeper handles the books. The bookkeeper does not own the money',
      'Decide, handle, or operate. Name which',
      'Controller decides. Processor handles', 'The vendor owns the employee data', 'Custodian means owner'),
    R(27, '5.2.identify', 'Risk identification',
      'Risk identification lists what could go wrong to assets you care about, from threats meeting weaknesses. A list that is only hackers is incomplete. Include mistakes, outages, suppliers, and storms',
      'The register includes ransomware, a lost laptop, a key supplier outage, and a flood. It does not include only malware. You review it when the business adds a new product',
      'A risk you refuse to name still exists',
      'inventory of worries', 'You write the real worries, including the dull ones',
      'Threats, mistakes, suppliers, and disasters',
      'More than hackers', 'Only malware', 'Unnamed risks do not count'),
    R(27, '5.2.assess', 'Assessment timing',
      'Assessments can be one-time, on a schedule, continuous, or ad hoc after a change. A one-time assessment at launch does not see the system you became. Continuous means you actually look, not that a tool is licensed',
      'You reassess after the merger adds a new subsidiary, not only on the annual date. The ad hoc look finds a domain nobody inventories. The annual meeting would have missed it for eleven months',
      'Ad hoc is not the same as never',
      'checkups', 'You do the annual physical and you also go in when something new hurts',
      'Scheduled, and when the business changes',
      'Reassess after real change', 'Launch day is enough forever', 'A license equals continuous'),
    R(27, '5.2.qual-quant', 'Qualitative and quantitative analysis',
      'Qualitative analysis uses ranks such as high and low. Quantitative analysis uses numbers: how often, how much money. Single loss expectancy is the cost of one event. Annualized rate is how often per year. Annualized loss multiplies them. Numbers without honest inputs are theater',
      'You estimate a laptop breach at a known notification cost and expect two a year. The product is a number you can compare to the cost of encryption. If the cost input is a guess, you say so',
      'A precise-looking number from a made-up input is worse than a clear high',
      'priced menu', 'The menu is useful if the prices are real',
      'Use numbers when the inputs are real. Say when they are not',
      'Show the inputs', 'A fake precise number', 'Only high and low, with no definition'),
    R(27, '5.2.likelihood', 'Likelihood, probability, impact, and exposure',
      'Likelihood or probability is how often you expect it. Impact is how bad one event is. Exposure factor is the portion of the asset you lose. A rare catastrophic event and a common small one can both matter. Write both',
      'A flood is rare and would destroy the only site. A lost laptop is common and smaller. The register keeps both. You do not drop the flood because the score looks unlikely',
      'Impact is not the same word as likelihood. Do not collapse them into one feeling',
      'storm and pothole', 'The storm is rare and huge. The pothole is weekly and small. You plan for both',
      'Keep frequency and harm separate',
      'Both the rare disaster and the common loss', 'Drop anything unlikely', 'One combined feeling'),
    R(27, '5.2.register', 'The risk register',
      'A risk register lists the risk, the owner, the score or rank, the treatment, and key indicators that would tell you it is getting worse. A threshold says when you must act. A register nobody opens is a souvenir',
      'The indicator for the phishing risk is the number of reported lures that were clicked. The threshold is agreed. When you cross it, the owner owes a change, not a new color on the slide',
      'A register without an owner is a list of fears',
      'workshop board', 'Each item has a name and a mark that means stop and fix',
      'Owner, treatment, indicator, threshold',
      'Crossing the threshold causes action', 'A slide with no names', 'Indicators nobody watches'),
    R(27, '5.2.appetite', 'Appetite and tolerance',
      'Appetite is how much risk leadership wants in pursuit of the mission: expansionary, conservative, or neutral. Tolerance is the amount they can live with on a given risk. Appetite is not a team slogan. It is a decision you can cite',
      'Leadership says the company is conservative on customer data and expansionary on a new public beta. You do not apply the beta appetite to payroll. You cite the decision',
      'Your personal appetite is not the company appetite',
      'speed of the ship', 'The captain sets the speed. The crew does not each pick one',
      'Cite the decision. Do not mix contexts',
      'Different appetites for different risks', 'One mood for everything', 'The loudest engineer decides'),
    R(27, '5.2.treat', 'Treat, tolerate, transfer, avoid',
      'Mitigate means add a control. Accept means live with it, sometimes as a formal exception or exemption. Transfer means share the impact, often by contract or insurance, and you still own the event. Avoid means stop the activity',
      'You cannot patch a device, so you avoid putting it on the internet, you mitigate with a segment, and you accept the residual risk in writing. Insurance transfers some money impact and does not remove the outage',
      'Accept is a written decision. It is not forgetting',
      'four choices at the fork', 'Fix it, live with it on paper, share the cost, or do not go',
      'Pick one and write it',
      'Accepting is written. Insurance is not a fix', 'Forget it and call that accept', 'Transfer means the vendor has the incident'),
    R(27, '5.2.report-risk', 'Risk reporting',
      'Risk reporting tells the people who can act: what changed, what is over threshold, and what decision you need. It is not a dump of every scanner line. The audience decides the altitude',
      'The board gets three risks over threshold and the decision needed on the legacy VPN. The operators get the work list. You do not paste the raw scan into the board pack',
      'A report that asks for nothing will get nothing',
      'briefing the captain', 'You bring the three things that need an order',
      'Decisions, not dumps',
      'Ask for the decision', 'The raw scan as the board pack', 'No change since last time, so silence even when you are over threshold'),
    R(27, '5.2.bia', 'Business impact analysis',
      'A business impact analysis asks which processes matter, how fast they must return, and how much data loss hurts. Recovery time and recovery point objectives come from it. So do the staffing and the technology you must actually have',
      'The analysis says the clinic schedule must return in four hours and can lose one hour of bookings. Backups are nightly, so they miss the point objective. You change the backups or you change the analysis in public',
      'IT does not invent these numbers alone. The process owner does',
      'which rooms matter', 'The hospital decides which rooms must reopen first',
      'The owner sets the numbers. The design meets them',
      'Match backups to the stated loss', 'IT invents the time limit', 'Every system is equally urgent'),

    R(28, '5.3.assess-vendor', 'Assessing a vendor',
      'A vendor assessment asks for evidence: a recent independent report, a penetration test summary, the right to audit, and how they treat their own suppliers. A glossy brochure is not evidence',
      'You ask for the latest independent report and the scope of their last penetration test. They offer a marketing PDF. You do not treat that as a pass. A right-to-audit clause is useless if you never have the skill to use it, so you decide when you would',
      'A questionnaire of only yes answers, unchecked, is not an assessment',
      'reference check', 'You call the references. You do not frame the advertisement',
      'Evidence, not a brochure',
      'Ask for a report and a scope', 'A marketing PDF passes', 'Yes on a form is proof'),
    R(28, '5.3.select', 'Vendor selection',
      'Selection compares the risk evidence with the business need. The cheapest vendor who cannot protect the data is not cheap. Write why you picked them and what residual risk you accepted',
      'Two tools meet the need. One will not say where data sits. You pick the other or you accept the gap in the register with an owner. Price was not the only column',
      'Selection is a decision record, not a calendar invite',
      'hiring', 'You hire on evidence, and you write the concern you accepted',
      'Record the residual risk',
      'Need, evidence, and the risk you accept', 'Cheapest wins silently', 'A demo is an assessment'),
    R(28, '5.3.agreements', 'Agreements',
      'Common papers: a service-level agreement on performance, a memorandum of understanding or agreement on intent, a master service agreement on the legal terms, a statement of work on the job, a non-disclosure agreement on secrets, and a business partners agreement on a joint effort. Use the one that matches the promise',
      'The vendor promises 99.9 percent uptime in a slide and the contract has no service level. You put the number in the contract or you stop quoting it. An NDA does not set uptime',
      'A friendly email is not one of these agreements',
      'which paper', 'A promise about money, a promise about secrets, and a promise about the job are different papers',
      'Put the promise in the right paper',
      'The contract holds the uptime, not the slide', 'An NDA covers uptime', 'A handshake is a service level'),
    R(28, '5.3.monitor-vendor', 'Monitoring vendors',
      'Vendor monitoring checks whether they still meet the agreement: reports, uptime, access lists, and news of their breaches. A vendor is not done when the contract is signed',
      'Your managed provider is breached and you hear it from the news, not from them. The contract required notice. You invoke it, you review their access in your environment, and you decide whether to suspend that access',
      'Annual questionnaires do not replace a breach-notice clause you actually watch',
      'landlord checks', 'You still walk the building after you sign the lease',
      'Notice, access review, and the contract',
      'Watch access and require notice', 'Signing ends the work', 'The news is not your problem'),
    R(28, '5.3.questions', 'Questionnaires and rules of engagement',
      'Questionnaires collect claims. You sample evidence behind the risky answers. Rules of engagement bound a test: targets, times, emergency contacts, and what must not be touched',
      'The penetration test rules forbid the payroll system and name a 24-hour phone. The tester hits payroll anyway. You stop the test. The questionnaire answer we encrypt is sampled by asking how the key is stored',
      'A questionnaire is a lead. Evidence is the assessment',
      'survey then spot check', 'You spot-check the answers that would hurt if they are wrong',
      'Sample evidence. Bound the test',
      'Rules name the off-limits systems', 'Yes means proven', 'Testers may roam'),
    R(28, '5.3.supply', 'Supply chain analysis',
      'Supply chain analysis looks past the vendor to their critical suppliers. Your risk includes the hosting they use and the code they bundle. Ask who they depend on',
      'The app vendor is healthy and their only hosting provider is having a week-long outage. Your service is down. You needed that dependency in the assessment and a continuity question in the contract',
      'The logo you pay is not the whole chain',
      'ingredients', 'You ask where the ingredients come from, not only who baked the bread',
      'Ask who they depend on',
      'Their suppliers are your risk', 'The contract party is the whole chain', 'Hosting is their secret'),
    R(28, '5.4.reporting', 'Internal and external compliance reporting',
      'Internal reporting tells leadership whether controls operate. External reporting tells a regulator, a customer, or an auditor. Both need evidence. A narrative without samples will not survive a question',
      'You report that encryption is on, and you attach the management-console sample. The external auditor asks for the sample. The sentence alone would have failed',
      'External reporting can create legal duties. Do not freelance a regulator letter',
      'two audiences', 'The family meeting and the inspector both want proof, in different words',
      'Evidence behind the sentence',
      'A sample, not only a claim', 'A narrative is enough', 'Email a regulator on your own'),
    R(28, '5.4.consequences', 'Consequences of non-compliance',
      'Consequences include fines, sanctions, reputational harm, loss of a license, and broken contracts. The point of naming them is to prioritize work, not to scare people for sport',
      'A failed control maps to a contract that lets the customer leave, not only to a theoretical fine. You fix that control ahead of a cosmetic policy edit. You do not announce a fine you do not know',
      'Reputation is real and it is not a number you invent',
      'lost permit', 'The shop can lose the permit, the customer, and the name',
      'Prioritize by real consequences',
      'Contract and license, not only a scary fine', 'Invent a fine amount', 'Consequences are someone else problem'),
    R(28, '5.4.monitor-comply', 'Compliance monitoring',
      'Monitoring compliance means due diligence before you commit and due care while you operate. Attestation is someone signing that a control is in place. Internal and external checks, sometimes automated, keep the signature honest',
      'Managers attest that laptops are encrypted. An automated sample finds exceptions. The attestation is now a conversation, not a shield. You fix the exceptions and you keep the sample',
      'An attestation without a sample is a ritual',
      'signed checklist', 'The signature plus a spot check, not the signature alone',
      'Spot-check what people attest',
      'Automation and samples', 'A signature is proof', 'Due care is only before the purchase'),
    R(28, '5.4.privacy', 'Privacy duties',
      'Privacy duties depend on the place: local, national, and global rules can all apply. A data subject is the person the data is about. Controllers decide. Processors handle. Ownership of the record is not the same as freedom to use it however you like',
      'A person asks for their data and for deletion. You verify who they are, you find the copies including the vendor, and you follow the rule that applies. You do not email them a raw database',
      'The right to be forgotten is not a slogan. It is a process with exceptions, such as a legal hold',
      'library card', 'The person can ask what you hold. A hold for a legal case can pause the shredder',
      'Verify, find the copies, follow the rule',
      'Include the vendor copies', 'Email the whole database', 'Deletion beats a legal hold'),
    R(28, '5.4.inventory-priv', 'Privacy inventory and retention',
      'A privacy inventory lists what personal data you have, why, where, and how long. Retention is that how long, applied to backups too. You cannot answer a subject request if you do not know where the data is',
      'Marketing cannot list the tools that hold contact details. You build the inventory before you promise a fast deletion. A tool missing from the list becomes the missed copy',
      'An inventory of servers is not an inventory of personal data',
      'map of the files', 'You cannot return a file you did not know you had',
      'What, why, where, how long',
      'List the data, not only the servers', 'We will search during the request', 'Backups are exempt from retention'),
    R(28, '5.5.attest', 'Attestation in audits',
      'In an audit, attestation is a formal claim, often by management or a third party, that controls exist. Auditors then test samples. Your job is to make the claim true before you sign, not to hope the sample misses',
      'You are asked to attest that access reviews happened. Two quarters are missing. You say so and you catch up. You do not sign the claim and hope',
      'A signature on a false claim is its own incident',
      'sworn statement', 'You do not swear the fence is closed if you have not walked it',
      'Make it true, then sign',
      'Refuse a false attestation', 'Sign and hope', 'Samples never happen'),
    R(28, '5.5.internal', 'Internal audits',
      'Internal audits can be compliance checks, audit-committee work, or self-assessments. They help you find gaps before an outsider does. A self-assessment that cannot say fail is not an assessment',
      'A self-assessment of backups finds that restores are untested. You write the fail and the fix. A culture that hides the fail will meet it again with an external auditor',
      'Internal does not mean optional or soft',
      'practice inspection', 'The practice inspection has to be allowed to fail',
      'Allow a real fail',
      'Write the fail and the fix', 'Internal means hide it', 'The committee only wants good news'),
    R(28, '5.5.external', 'External audits and exams',
      'External work includes regulatory exams, independent assessments, and third-party audits. They produce findings you must track to a fix. Arguing the wording is not the same as closing the finding',
      'An external audit finds shared admin passwords. You track a finding to the vault rollout with a date. A response that says we disagree, with no change, leaves the finding open',
      'The report is an input to the register, not a trophy or an insult',
      'outside inspector', 'You fix the cited item. You do not only debate the sentence',
      'Track findings to a change',
      'A dated fix, not only a rebuttal', 'Disagree and close it', 'Hide the report'),
    R(28, '5.5.pentest-types', 'Kinds of penetration tests',
      'Tests can be physical, offensive, defensive, or integrated. The environment can be known, partly known, or unknown to the tester. Reconnaissance is passive when it does not touch your systems and active when it does. The rules of engagement say which',
      'A physical test tries to walk in. An unknown-environment test starts with no map. Passive reconnaissance reads public information. Active reconnaissance scans you and needs permission. You do not call a surprise scan a passive look',
      'Defensive testing checks whether detections fire. Offensive testing tries to reach a goal. Integrated does both',
      'kinds of drills', 'A fire drill, a lock test, and a stranger with no map are different exercises',
      'Name the kind and what they were allowed to touch',
      'Permission for anything active', 'A surprise scan is passive', 'Physical tests need no rules'),
    R(28, '5.6.phish-program', 'Phishing programs',
      'A phishing program includes realistic campaigns, a way to recognize lures, and a response when someone reports or clicks. The goal is reports, not a leaderboard of shame',
      'A campaign uses a current lure theme. People who report are thanked. People who click get a short lesson and no public list. You measure time-to-report, not only click rate',
      'A gotcha campaign that punishes reporters will end the reports',
      'practice alarm', 'You praise the person who pulls the alarm during practice',
      'Teach, thank, and measure reports',
      'No public shame list', 'Publish the clickers', 'One campaign means you are done'),
    R(28, '5.6.behavior', 'Recognizing risky behavior',
      'Anomalous behavior can be risky on purpose, unexpected, or unintentional. The response differs. A malicious insider, a compromised account, and a tired mistake do not get the same first conversation, but they can get the same containment',
      'A large download might be a project or a theft. You contain the sharing if it is still going, and you ask the manager before you accuse. Unintentional mis-sends get a process fix, not a villain story',
      'Calling every mistake malicious hides the control you should add',
      'three reasons for a spilled paint', 'On purpose, a bump, or a broken shelf. You still mop',
      'Contain first if needed. Do not rush the motive',
      'Separate motive from containment', 'Every anomaly is a firing', 'Unintentional means ignore it'),
    R(28, '5.6.training', 'What awareness training covers',
      'Training covers the policies, situational awareness, insider risk, passwords, removable media and unknown cables, social engineering, operational security, and how hybrid or remote work changes the risks. It is short, repeated, and tied to real tickets',
      'After a run of USB drops, the next module is that story, in ten minutes, with the procedure. A yearly hour on generic threats would have missed the moment',
      'Operational security means not posting the internal map, the outage, or the badge photo',
      'toolbox talk', 'The talk is about the hazard on this site this week',
      'Short, current, and specific',
      'Use this month incidents', 'A yearly generic hour only', 'Remote work has no extra risks'),
    R(28, '5.6.reporting-aw', 'Reporting and metrics for awareness',
      'Initial training happens at joining. Recurring training happens on a schedule and after incidents. You track completion and, better, whether reports of suspicious messages go up. Completion alone is a weak metric',
      'Completion is 100 percent and nobody has reported a lure in six months. You treat that as a problem. You would rather see reports. You do not buy a harder test just to punish',
      'A metric that people game by clicking through slides is not awareness',
      'fire reports', 'You want more pulled alarms that are real, not a perfect attendance sheet',
      'Measure reports, not only completion',
      'Reports are a success', '100 percent completion is the goal', 'Skip recurring training'),
    R(28, '5.6.follow', 'Closing the loop on a report',
      'When someone reports a lure or a mistake, close the loop: thank them, say what you did, and fix the control if there is one. A mailbox that swallows reports trains people to stop sending them',
      'A user reports a wire-change email. You confirm it was fake, you tell them the payment process caught it, and you add the look-alike domain to the block. They hear back the same day',
      'A ticket closed with no reply is a missed sensor',
      'answer the radio', 'You answer the person who called it in, or they stop calling',
      'Reply, and fix what you can',
      'Same-day thanks and the action taken', 'Close the ticket in silence', 'Only reply if they were wrong'),
    R(28, '5.6.execute', 'Developing and running the program',
      'Development is building the content and the process. Execution is actually running it, including the report button, the thank-you, and the fix from the last lesson. A program that is only content is a library',
      'You have modules and no report button in the mail client. You add the button, you staff the mailbox, and you close the loop with the person who reports. That is execution',
      'Buying content is development help. It is not execution',
      'drill versus poster', 'The poster is content. The drill is execution',
      'Run the loop: report, thank, fix',
      'A staffed report path', 'Content on a shelf', 'A report mailbox nobody reads'),
]
